Check out the google video by Rick Hudson ( its a very good explanation of TLO-CC )

The summary of it being there are 8 rules to keep in mind:

T0                     T1                      Reodered?

Load		       Store		       NO

Load		       Load		       NO

Store		       Store		       NO

Store		       Load		       YES (store buffering unless they re to the same address AKA Causal Consistency)

Lock		       Load		       NO

Lock		       Store		       NO


Keep in mind a lot of legacy systems implement LockRelease using simple store instructions. Having an early Load or Store
swap places with a later Store allows Loads/Stores to slip outside critical sections, not an option.

So the real reordering that a processor could pull below your feet would be letting reads bypass writes when writes are 
added to write buffers.

Total Ordering ( meaning all processors see the same order ):

<a> All lock instructions ( even if they dont use the same memory )

<b> Stores to the same memory location 

Hence the name, TLO-CC (Total Lock Order - Causal Consistency)